To solve for the imaginary part of $\overline{z}$, where $z=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{\sqrt{2}+i}-\frac{i}{2}$, we follow these steps:

1. Simplify $z$ by rationalizing the denominator of the first term and combining it with the second term:
\begin{align*}
z &= \frac{\sqrt{2}}{\sqrt{2}+i}-\frac{i}{2} \\
&= \frac{\sqrt{2}(\sqrt{2}-i)}{(\sqrt{2}+i)(\sqrt{2}-i)}-\frac{1}{2}i \\
&= \frac{2-\sqrt{2}i}{3}-\frac{1}{2}i \\
&= \frac{2}{3}-\left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{3}+\frac{1}{2}\right)i.
\end{align*}

2. To find $\overline{z}$, we take the conjugate of $z$, which involves changing the sign of the imaginary part:
\begin{align*}
\overline{z} &= \frac{2}{3}+\left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{3}+\frac{1}{2}\right)i.
\end{align*}

3. The imaginary part of $\overline{z}$ is therefore:
\begin{align*}
\text{Imaginary part of }\overline{z} &= \frac{\sqrt{2}}{3}+\frac{1}{2}.
\end{align*}

Hence, the imaginary part of $\overline{z}$ is $\boxed{\frac{\sqrt{2}}{3}+\frac{1}{2}}$.

